AB  -  30 case of Bell's palsy were included in the study. During 13 days of drug treatment, in 10 cases low doses and gradually decreasing amount while in 10 cases alternate day high doses of steroid (dexamethasone) and in 10 cases placebo only were given. Severity of facial paralysis before treatment and degree of improvement in 7 th and 21 st days after treatment were measured in terms of muscular power of frontal, ocular and buccinator muscles. The aim of the study was to compare the results of steroid and placebo treatments in cases of Bell's palsy. This study demonstrated that there was no difference between steroid and placebo in the treatment of Bell's palsy. 25 out of 30 cases of acute facial paralysis exhibited improvement in 2‐5 weeks regardless of the drug given. In 5 cases who showed slow recovery, there was hyperacusia, disturbance of taste and dryness of the mouth. Pregnancy and hypertension seemed contributory factors responsible for delayed recovery.
